.TH LORARAW 1
.SH NAME
loraraw \- a RadioHead peer built by hand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loraraw hear
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B loraraw poke
.I to from id flags text
.br
.B loraraw ackpeer
.I me
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B loraraw deaf
.I me
.RI [ n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Loraraw
writes and reads RadioHead frames directly on
.BR /dev/radio0/data ,
building the four bytes
.PP
.EX
	to from id flags
.EE
.PP
from the specification rather than from
.BR /os/inferno/radio/devlora.c .
Address 255 is broadcast,
.B 0x80
is the acknowledge flag,
.B 0x40
the retry flag, and the payload of an acknowledgement is
.BR ! .
.PP
It exists because
.IR lorachk (1)
checks
.IR lora (3)
against itself, which cannot catch a header this tree agrees with and
nobody else does.  This is not somebody else's program, which is its
weakness; it is somebody else's format, written on the other side of a
device boundary with none of
.IR lora (3)'s
code reachable from it, which is as far as that can be taken without a
second radio.
.PP
.B Hear
prints frames as they arrive.
.B Poke
sends one, exactly as given, with no checking: an oversize
.I id
or a flag nobody defines goes out as written, because the point is to
be able to send what a correct implementation would not.
.PP
.B Ackpeer
answers anything addressed to
.I me
the way RadioHead does \- the sender's address, the same identifier,
the ACK flag and
.B ! .
.B Deaf
takes frames and says nothing, which is what makes the other end
retransmit; it then checks that the identifier did not change and that
the retry flag was clear on the first attempt and set on every one
after, and prints
.B RAWDEAF-OK
if so.  That is the check no single implementation can make against
itself.
.SH EXAMPLE
Two stations under a QEMU patched to model an SX1276.  On the first,
.IP
.EX
lorachk setup 2
lorachk ackrecv 1
.EE
.PP
and on the second,
.IP
.EX
loraraw poke 2 1 77 0 hand-built
loraraw hear 1
.EE
.PP
The first prints the payload; the second prints
.PP
.EX
	RAW to 1 from 2 id 77 flags 0x80 payload !
.EE
.PP
which is the acknowledgement, to the byte.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/loraraw.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I lorachk (1),
.I lora (3),
.I radio (3)
.SH BUGS
It speaks only to
.B /dev/radio0/data
and there is no way to point it at another packet device.
.PP
.B Deaf
counts frames addressed to
.I me
and ignores the rest, so a busy channel makes its count mean less than
it looks.
